    const Section(
      title: '+ Classify the types of brachial plexus birth injury, explain the relevant nerve root levels, and discuss your approach to prognostication including the role of imaging and electrodiagnostics.',
      content: '**Classification of Neonatal Brachial Plexus Palsy**:\n\n1. **Erb Palsy (C5-C6 — Upper Trunk)**: Most common (approximately 70-80% of cases). Affects shoulder abduction, external rotation, elbow flexion, forearm supination. The \"waiter\'s tip\" posture. Prognosis is generally favorable — 70-90% recover spontaneously if recovery begins by 2-3 months.\n\n2. **Extended Erb Palsy (C5-C6-C7 — Upper and Middle Trunk)**: Adds wrist and finger extension weakness to the Erb pattern. More guarded prognosis.\n\n3. **Klumpke Palsy (C8-T1 — Lower Trunk)**: Rare in isolation (< 1%). Affects hand intrinsic muscles, finger flexors, wrist flexors. \"Claw hand\" deformity. Often associated with Horner syndrome if T1 sympathetic fibers are involved.\n\n4. **Total Plexus Palsy (C5-T1)**: Complete flail arm. Worst prognosis. Often involves avulsion injuries. May have Horner syndrome and phrenic nerve palsy.\n\n**This Patient**: Right C5-C6 (Erb palsy). Intact hand function (C8-T1 spared). No Horner syndrome. Favorable pattern.\n\n**Prognostication**:\n- **Most important prognostic indicator**: Recovery of **antigravity biceps function (elbow flexion) by 3-6 months of age**. If biceps recovers by 3 months, prognosis for good functional outcome is excellent. If no biceps recovery by 5-6 months, microsurgical exploration is indicated.\n- **Toronto Test Score**: At 3 months, assess AMS scores across multiple muscle groups. A total score helps predict who will and will not recover.\n\n**Imaging**:\n- **Chest X-ray**: Rule out phrenic nerve palsy (elevated right hemidiaphragm) and clavicle fracture.\n- **MRI neurography of the brachial plexus**: Can identify nerve root avulsion (pseudomeningocele = pathognomonic for avulsion), but requires sedation/anesthesia in an infant. Reserve for surgical planning if no recovery by 3-6 months.\n- **Ultrasound**: Emerging role for visualizing neuroma-in-continuity. Non-invasive and does not require sedation.\n\n**Electrodiagnostics (EMG/N