      content: '**Immediate Rehabilitation Program (0-3 months)**:\n\n1. **Gentle passive range of motion (PROM)**: Parents taught by a pediatric therapist to perform gentle PROM of the right shoulder (especially external rotation and abduction), elbow (flexion/extension), and forearm (supination) multiple times daily. **Critical to prevent contracture** — internal rotation contracture is the most common secondary deformity.\n\n2. **Positioning**: Encourage midline orientation. Place toys to the right side to stimulate visual tracking toward the affected arm. Avoid traction on the arm (careful with dressing, bathing, lifting under arms).\n\n3. **Sensory stimulation**: Tactile input to the affected arm (gentle massage, textured toys) to maintain cortical representation and promote neuroplasticity.\n\n4. **Parent education**: Teach safe handling techniques — **never pull the affected arm**. Demonstrate proper lifting (support under trunk, not under arms).\n\n**Monitoring Protocol (3-9 months)**:\n- **Monthly clinic visits** with Active Movement Scale (AMS) scoring to track recovery objectively.\n- **Critical decision point at 3 months**: If biceps function is absent (AMS < 3 for elbow flexion), refer to a brachial plexus microsurgeon for evaluation.\n- **Cookie test (Tassin test)**: At 3 months, observe if the infant can bring the hand to the mouth. Inability to flex the elbow against gravity is the strongest indicator for surgical intervention.\n- **If recovery is progressing**: Continue conservative management with therapy.\n\n**Surgical Intervention (if indicated at 3-6 months)**:\n- **Microsurgical nerve reconstruction**: Nerve grafting (sural nerve autograft), nerve transfer (Oberlin transfer — ulnar nerve fascicle to biceps motor nerve for elbow flexion), or neurolysis of neuroma-in-continuity.\n- Optimal surgical window is **3-9 months of age**. Earlier surgery allows reinnervation before motor endplate degeneration (which occurs by approximately 12-18 months).\n\n**Splinting**:\n- **Shoulder abduction splint**: Consider if developing internal rotation contracture. \"Sully\" or custom-molded orthosis to maintain the shoulder in neutral to slight external rotation and abduction.\n\n**Later Interventions (if residual deficits)**:\n- **Botulinum toxin (age 1-4 years)**: To the internal rotators (pectoralis major, subscapularis) if muscle imbalance develops, to allow external rotators to strengthen. Combine with intensive OT.\n- **Secondary orthopedic procedures (age 2-8 years)**: Tendon transfers (latissimus dorsi/teres major transfer to rotator cuff for external rotation), humeral osteotomy for fixed internal rotation contracture, subscapularis release.',
